description:
FOUND s496a (Opus, 2026-09-26) while measuring #2372 on the companion.

A code block used as a regex CONDITION -- `(?(?{ CODE })YES|NO)` -- cannot be handled by the code-block STRIP (%pcl-strip-regex-code-blocks removes `(?{ CODE })` and leaves `(?YES|NO)`, which is not a pattern).  Before #2372: a warning and a no-match; since #2372: a DIE at the first match (cl-ppcre: "Character ' ' may not follow '(?'").  perl evaluates CODE and takes a branch.

Minimal reproducer:
    my $c = 1;
    my $r = eval { ("ab" =~ /a(?(?{ $c })b|c)/) ? "match" : "no" } // "died";
    print "$r\n";
  perl: match      PCL b6560c21: no (+ warning)      PCL s496a: died

COST MEASURED: t/re/pat.t's top-level `(?(?{ $c }) ...` pattern aborts the file: C_ok 245 -> 206.

This is the #874 / "Regex code blocks" family (docs/not-supported.md): PCL has no mid-match callback into perl.  A condition whose CODE is a plain variable could be evaluated ONCE at compile time only if nothing in the pattern can change it -- not generally true.  Until code blocks exist, the die is the honest answer; the strip should at least make the error name the construct (e.g. signal "regex code-block CONDITION (?(?{...})) is not supported") instead of cl-ppcre's position in a pattern the program never wrote.
